= The Devolution Question

The government's Scotland and Wales Bills are crawling through the Commons. Once passed, the Acts will go to referendums, and a Labour backbencher's amendment says the Scottish Assembly needs the votes of 40% of the whole electorate.

The Conservative Party was once committed to a Scottish Assembly. Many of our own Scots still are; our Unionist allies and much of the right are not.

Current position: [? if devolution_stance = 0 : opposed ?][? if devolution_stance = 1 : supportive ?][? if devolution_stance = 2 : "No, for a better Bill" ?]

- @oppose: Defend the Union and oppose the Acts.
- @support: Back the Scottish Assembly.
- @better_bill: Vote No, and promise a better Bill.
- @easy_discard

@oppose
on-arrival: devolution_stance = 0; right_dissent -= 3; uup_relation += 3; snp_relation -= 5; wet_dissent += 2

We stand for the Union. Some pro-devolution Scottish Tories, like Malcolm Rifkind and Alick Buchanan-Smith, are uneasy.

@support
on-arrival: devolution_stance = 1; snp_relation += 10; right_dissent += 8; uup_relation -= 5; scotland_con_bonus += 0.5; wet_dissent -= 3

We back the Assembly in principle. The SNP is wrong-footed, and the right of the party is furious.

@better_bill
on-arrival: devolution_stance = 2; snp_relation -= 3; right_dissent -= 1; wet_dissent -= 1; scotland_con_bonus += 0.5

We follow Lord Home's line: vote No to this flawed Act, and a Conservative government will bring forward something better. Whether we would is another question.
